<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gallipoli campaign</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1915–1916</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Dardanelles campaign · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallipoli">Gallipoli</a> campaign was an Allied attempt in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> to force the Dardanelles and knock the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> out of the war. British, French, Australian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> troops landed on the Gallipoli peninsula in April 1915 but were held by Ottoman forces, including those under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>, and withdrew by January 1916 after heavy losses. The landings became a founding national memory in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and New Zealand, marked on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anzac-day">Anzac Day</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gallipoli-campaign.jpg" alt="Gallipoli campaign" width="320" height="435" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: LoudHmen · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:G.C._18_March_1915_Gallipoli_Campaign_Article.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
